# Code owners — review gate for the files that exist to catch regressions.
#
# Companion to .github/workflows/test-integrity.yml. That workflow makes a
# protected-path change FAIL; this makes it require the maintainer's review
# before it can merge at all. Two independent mechanisms, because PR #172
# showed that a single guard whose evidence lives in the repo is not enough:
# the dependency-floor test was working correctly and was simply out-voted by
# an edit to the baseline JSON it reads.
#
# CODEOWNERS only has teeth when branch protection has
# "Require review from Code Owners" enabled on main / Rawhide / dev / *-Beta.

# Default: everything else can be reviewed normally.
*                                   @linux4life1

# ── The regression net itself ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Editing any of these is editing the evidence, not the code.
/test/                              @linux4life1
/integration_test/                  @linux4life1
/test/deps/dependency_floors.json   @linux4life1

# ── CI, and the config that decides what CI even checks ──────────────────
# A PR that can rewrite the workflows can grant itself a pass.
/.github/                           @linux4life1
/analysis_options.yaml              @linux4life1

# ── Release + packaging: a bad edit here ships to every user at once ─────
/scripts/                           @linux4life1
/pubspec.yaml                       @linux4life1
/pubspec.lock                       @linux4life1

# ── Schema: an external tool (Character Card Forge) writes these tables
#    with raw SQL, so a migration change can break software we do not ship.
/lib/database/                      @linux4life1
